30. ''Toroidal Functions.''—For potential problems connected with the anchor-ring, the following co-ordinates are appropriate: If <math>\text{A}</math>, <math>\text{B}</math> are points at the extremities of a diameter of a fixed circle, and <math>\text{P}</math> is any point in the plane <math>\text{PAB}</math> which is perpendicular to the plane of the fixed circle, let <math>\text{P} = \log(\text{AP}/\text{BP})</math>, <math>\theta = \angle \text{APB}</math>, and let <math>\phi</math> be the angle the plane <math>\text{APB}</math> makes with a fixed plane through the axis of the circle. Let <math>\theta</math> be restricted to lie between <math>-\pi</math> and <math>\pi</math>, a

readings.c2s3:
discontinuity in its value arising as we pass through the circle, so that within the circumference <math>\theta</math> is <math>\pi</math> on the upper side of the circle, and <math>-\pi</math> on the lower side; <math>\theta</math> is zero in the plane of the circle outside the circumference; <math>\rho</math> may have any value between <math>-\infty</math> and <math>\infty</math>, and <math>\phi</math> any value between 0 and <math>2\pi</math>. The position of a point is then uniquely represented by the co-ordinates <math>\rho</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math>, which are the parameters of a system of tores with the fixed circle as limiting circle, a system of bowls with the fixed circle as common rim, and a system of planes through the axis of the tores. If <math>x</math>, <math>y</math>, <math>z</math> are the co-ordinates of a point referred to axes, two of which <math>x</math>, <math>y</math> are in the plane of the circle and the third along its axis, we find that
{{block center|<math>x = \frac{a \sinh \rho}{\cosh \rho - \cos \theta} \cos \phi,\ y = \frac{a \sinh \rho}{\cosh \rho - \cos \theta} \sin \phi,\ z = \frac{a \sin \theta}{\cosh \rho - \cos \theta},</math>}}
where <math>a</math> is the radius of the fixed circle.

Laplace's equation reduces to
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial}{\partial \rho} \left\{ \frac{\sinh \rho}{\text{P}^2} \frac{\partial \text{V}}{\partial \rho} \right\} + \frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \left\{ \frac{\sinh \rho}{\text{P}^2} \frac{\partial \text{V}}{\partial \theta} \right\} + \frac{1}{\text{P}^2 \sinh \rho} \frac{\partial^2 \text{V}}{\partial \phi^2} = 0,</math>}}
when <math>\text{P}</math> denotes <math>\sqrt{(\cosh \rho - \cos \theta)}</math>. It can be shown that this equation is satisfied by
{{block center|<math>\sqrt{(\cosh \rho - \cos \theta)} \begin{matrix} \text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho) \cos \\ \text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho) \sin \end{matrix} n\theta \begin{matrix} \cos \\ \sin \end{matrix} m\phi,</math>}}
the functions <math>\text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho)</math>, <math>\text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho)</math> required for the potential problems, are associated Legendre's functions of degree <math>n-\tfrac{1}{2}</math>, half an odd integer, of integral order <math>m</math>, and of argument real and greater than unity; these are known as toroidal functions. For the space external to a boundary tore the function <math>\text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho)</math> must be used, and for the internal space <math>\text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh \rho)</math>.
